// virtual loopback NIC - the lowest layer of this project's toy network
// stack (ip.bl0, udp.bl0 build on top of this). There is no real host
// networking underneath: "transmitting" a frame here loops it straight
// back into this same VM's own receive queue, the same way a real
// loopback interface (lo) behaves on an actual machine, just without a
// real kernel/driver underneath it. A frame is a plain array of ints, each
// 0-255, one byte - the shape every layer above this one builds and
// parses.
//
// Modeled with the same interrupt-driven shape as kernel.bl0's Keyboard
// driver: Nic.send() queues the frame on a TX queue and raises an
// interrupt; onNicIRQ() (the "device"'s own ISR) is what actually moves
// everything queued into the RX queue Nic.recv() drains from - not
// Nic.send() itself. The TX side is a real queue, not a single slot: an
// earlier single-slot version silently lost a frame whenever two cores
// sent before the ISR ran (the second overwrote the first), and with no
// retransmission above this layer that showed up as an occasionally hung
// TCP handshake in multi-core runs.
//
// Unlike out8()/in8()/registerHandler() (all privileged - see
// requirePrivileged's own doc), Nic.send()/Nic.hasFrame()/Nic.recv() are
// deliberately NOT gated behind a syscall, the same reasoning kernel.bl0's
// Keyboard driver already applies to kbHasInput()/kbReadChar(): these only
// ever touch a plain class field and call the already-ungated
// raiseInterrupt() native, never a raw port write - there's no privileged
// primitive underneath to protect here, so gating it would be theater, not
// an actual boundary. Nic.init() (which DOES call the privileged
// registerHandler()) is the one function in this file real kernel code
// still has to run before dropping to user mode.
//
// lock guards every read/write of txQueue/rxQueue - real hardware, and
// every other device driver in aeon-os, only ever has ONE core polling it
// (see smp_boot.bl0's own header comment: userspace tasks are real worker
// threads, but this VM has no per-core NIC, just one shared one). Once a
// client and a server both live in the same process (dispatch()'d onto
// different cores - see stdlib/net/tcp.bl0/http.bl0), TWO real threads can
// call Nic.send()/Nic.recv() at once, and rxQueue is a compound structure
// (an array) - exactly the case smp_boot.bl0's own Scheduler.tasks already
// established needs a real Mutex, not just care: disableInterrupts()
// masks interrupt DELIVERY on one core, it does nothing for a data race
// between two cores that were never interrupted in the first place.
// --- host bridging: a real UDP (later TCP) socket on the host, relayed
// through here so this stack can talk to a REAL external program (curl,
// netcat, a Python test client) instead of only ever looping back to
// itself - see the CLI's own -b/--bridge-udp flag for how it's attached.
// The frame FORMAT crossing this boundary is still this project's OWN
// fake IP/UDP framing (ip.bl0/udp.bl0), not a real Ethernet/IP frame - the
// bridge translates between "real java.net socket semantics" (a payload
// plus a separately-tracked real sender address) and this stack's own
// wire format, on both sides. Modeled as a second pair of ports, parallel
// to the loopback path above, not a replacement for it: Nic.send() keeps
// looping back to itself even with a bridge attached (see its own doc) -
// a real NIC doesn't do that, but nothing here depends on it NOT
// happening either, and losing loopback would break every existing
// single-process demo (aeon-os/shell.bl0 and friends, this stack's own
// test suite) for the sake of a purity real hardware doesn't even need
// enforced at this layer.
//
// RX (host -> VM): the bridge thread calls hostPortWrite() to place a
// just-arrived real packet's length + bytes + a bumped sequence number on
// these ports, then raiseInterrupt(Nic.hostRxVector) - the same write-
// then-signal pairing hostPortWrite()'s own doc describes for the
// keyboard, plus a sequence number since this side needs the same
// consumed-before-overwritten guarantee TX has (see onHostNicIRQ()'s own
// doc): the bridge blocks on hostRxAckPort matching what it just wrote
// before injecting anything else - on a real bridge, more than one Java
// thread can be injecting for the SAME connection (a real client's own
// bytes on one thread, this VM's own ACKs for what it just sent on
// another - see TcpBridge's own doc), so without this a second injection
// can land on top of the first before onHostNicIRQ() ever reads it. TX
// (VM -> host): Nic.hostSend() writes a frame's length + bytes here and
// bumps a sequence number; the bridge thread POLLS hostPortRead() on the
// sequence port (there's no "host wants to know about a write" callback -
// see hostPortRead()'s own doc) and only relays out when it changes, then
// writes the seq it just consumed back to hostTxAckPort. Nic.hostSend()
// blocks on that ack before returning - the single TX slot above has no
// queue behind it, so two sends close together (a reply immediately
// followed by tcpClose()'s own FIN, say) would otherwise race: the second
// write can land before the bridge's poll cycle ever observes the first,
// silently overwriting it. Mirrors how a real PIO-mode NIC's transmit
// path works - write the descriptor, then wait for a "transmit complete"
// status bit - not fire-and-forget.
//
// onNicIRQ/onHostNicIRQ stay plain top-level functions, NOT Nic static
// methods, even though everything else below became one - they're passed
// BY REFERENCE to registerHandler(), and a static method has no bare
// callable-value form in this language (only ClassName.method(args), an
// immediate call, ever compiles - see Bl0jv2_Compiler's own
// staticTargetOf() handling of a bare FieldAccessNode). Every other
// function here is always called directly, never passed around as a
// value, so those became Nic's own static methods without that
// restriction applying.
def class Nic {
    static field vector;
    static field txQueue;  // frames sent but not yet moved to rxQueue by the ISR
    static field rxQueue;  // already-delivered frames; the live ones start at rxHead
    static field rxHead;   // index of the oldest unread frame (popping advances this instead of copying the array)
    static field lock;
    static field dropNext; // test hook / lossy-link simulation: silently discard the next N sent frames
    static field txCount;  // frames sent / received (not counting ones dropped by Nic.dropNext)
    static field rxCount;
    static field hostBridgePresentPort;
    static field hostSendVector;
    static field rxEvent;  // signalled whenever the network state may have changed (a frame queued, a TCP segment handled) - see recvWait()

    static field hostBridgeEnabled;
    static field maxFrameBytes;
    static field hostRxVector;
    static field hostRxLenPort;
    static field hostRxDataBase;
    static field hostRxSeqPort;
    static field hostRxAckPort;
    static field hostTxSeqPort;
    static field hostTxLenPort;
    static field hostTxDataBase;
    static field hostTxAckPort;
    static field txSeq;

    // call once, before dropping to user mode - same "not at import time"
    // rule every other aeon-os driver init function follows (see
    // kernel.bl0's initKeyboard() for why)
    static def init() {
        registerHandler(onNicIRQ, Nic.vector, 50);
    }

    // call instead of init() when a host bridge will be attached (the
    // CLI's own -b/--bridge-udp flag - see this file's own header
    // comment). Registers BOTH ISRs - loopback keeps working even with a
    // bridge attached, see this file's own header comment on why
    static def initWithHostBridge() {
        Nic.init();
        registerHandler(onHostNicIRQ, Nic.hostRxVector, 50);
        // sending to the host writes ports (privileged); this syscall lets user-mode
        // code do it through the kernel - see send()
        registerHandler(onHostSendSyscall, Nic.hostSendVector, 1);
        Nic.hostBridgeEnabled = true;
    }

    // init() or initWithHostBridge(), whichever fits what is on the other side:
    // the host sets a port when it attaches any bridge (the CLI's -b / --bridge-tcp /
    // --bridge-outbound), so a program need not know how it was launched. Without
    // a bridge, hostSend() would wait out its 2 s ack timeout for every frame.
    // Privileged (reads a port, registers handlers) - call it before dropToUserMode().
    static def initAuto() {
        if (in16(Nic.hostBridgePresentPort) == 1) {
            Nic.initWithHostBridge();
        } else {
            Nic.init();
        }
    }

    // mirrors 'frame' out through the TX ports for a host bridge to relay
    // onto a real socket - privileged (out8/out16 are raw port writes,
    // gated the same as any other hardware access - see
    // requirePrivileged's own doc), unlike Nic.send() itself. Silently
    // truncates a frame bigger than Nic.maxFrameBytes rather than
    // throwing - a toy safeguard against overrunning the fixed port
    // window, not real fragmentation.
    static def hostSend(frame) {
        n = len(frame);
        if (n > Nic.maxFrameBytes) { n = Nic.maxFrameBytes; }
        i = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            out8(Nic.hostTxDataBase + i, frame[i]);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        out16(Nic.hostTxLenPort, n);
        Nic.txSeq = Nic.txSeq + 1;
        out16(Nic.hostTxSeqPort, Nic.txSeq);

        // block until the bridge has actually consumed THIS seq before
        // returning - see this file's own header comment on why the
        // single TX slot needs this. Bounded, not infinite: a bridge
        // that's gone (closed, or never attached even though
        // hostBridgeEnabled somehow got set) must not hang the caller
        // forever - 2 seconds is generous for a local poll loop that
        // checks every few milliseconds.
        i = 0;
        while (in16(Nic.hostTxAckPort) != Nic.txSeq && i < 2000) {
            wait(1);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // unchanged from before a bridge could ever be attached (still
    // ungated - see this file's own header comment on why loopback itself
    // needs no privilege check), except for one addition: relays out to
    // the host too when a bridge is actually attached. That branch alone
    // requires privilege (Nic.hostSend() calls out8/out16) - calling
    // Nic.send() from user mode is only a problem once a bridge is
    // attached AND enough of this stack's own privilege story has been
    // threaded down to userspace to matter, neither of which any demo in
    // this project does yet (see Nic.hostSend()'s own doc); a real kernel
    // would gate this the same way kernel.bl0's Console gates out8 behind
    // syscall() instead, which is exactly where this is heading, not a
    // finished design yet.
    static def send(frame) {
        if (Nic.dropNext > 0) {
            Nic.dropNext = Nic.dropNext - 1;
            return nil;
        }
        lock(Nic.lock);
        push(Nic.txQueue, frame);
        Nic.txCount = Nic.txCount + 1;
        unlock(Nic.lock);
        raiseInterrupt(Nic.vector);
        if (Nic.hostBridgeEnabled) {
            if (isPrivileged()) {
                Nic.hostSend(frame);
            } else {
                syscall(Nic.hostSendVector, frame); // the kernel's handler (onHostSendSyscall) does the port writes
            }
        }
    }

    // number of frames waiting to be received
    static def pending() {
        lock(Nic.lock);
        result = len(Nic.rxQueue) - Nic.rxHead;
        unlock(Nic.lock);
        return result;
    }

    static def hasFrame() {
        return Nic.pending() > 0;
    }

    // FIFO: pops the OLDEST frame, not the newest. Popping advances rxHead (and
    // clears the slot) - it used to rebuild the whole array shifted down by
    // one, O(queue length) per frame. Once everything has been read the array
    // is replaced by an empty one. Caller must check hasFrame() first, same
    // contract kbReadChar() already has for an empty keyboard buffer - fine
    // under single-core use, but with two real cores polling, hasFrame() and
    // recv() are two separate locked sections, so another core can still drain
    // the queue in between them; use tryRecv() instead of this pair when that
    // matters.
    static def recv() {
        lock(Nic.lock);
        frame = Nic.popLocked();
        unlock(Nic.lock);
        return frame;
    }

    // the pop itself; the caller holds Nic.lock and has checked the queue is not empty
    static def popLocked() {
        frame = Nic.rxQueue[Nic.rxHead];
        Nic.rxQueue[Nic.rxHead] = nil;
        Nic.rxHead = Nic.rxHead + 1;
        if (Nic.rxHead == len(Nic.rxQueue)) {
            Nic.rxQueue = [];
            Nic.rxHead = 0;
        }
        return frame;
    }

    // blocking receive: sleeps on Nic.rxEvent until a frame is queued, with
    // no polling - the RX ISRs below signal it right after pushing. Returns
    // the oldest frame, or nil if timeoutMs elapsed first (a negative
    // timeout waits forever). Safe from any core: the actual pop is
    // tryRecv()'s atomic one. The event's generation is read BEFORE the
    // queue check, so a frame that lands between the check and the wait
    // still ends the wait immediately instead of being slept through -
    // and the loop re-checks after every wakeup, because waitEvent() only
    // means "something happened" (another core may have taken the frame,
    // or a pending interrupt cut the wait short).
    static def recvWait(timeoutMs) {
        deadline = ticks() + timeoutMs;
        while (true) {
            gen = eventGen(Nic.rxEvent);
            frame = Nic.tryRecv();
            if (frame != nil) { return frame; }
            if (timeoutMs < 0) {
                waitEvent(Nic.rxEvent, gen, -1);
            } else {
                remaining = deadline - ticks();
                if (remaining <= 0) { return nil; }
                waitEvent(Nic.rxEvent, gen, remaining);
            }
        }
    }

    // atomic "pop the oldest frame if there is one" - nil if the queue was
    // empty. Safe to call from multiple cores with no separate has-frame
    // check racing against the pop itself, unlike hasFrame()+recv() as two
    // separate calls (see recv()'s own doc)
    static def tryRecv() {
        lock(Nic.lock);
        if (len(Nic.rxQueue) == Nic.rxHead) {
            unlock(Nic.lock);
            return nil;
        }
        frame = Nic.popLocked();
        unlock(Nic.lock);
        return frame;
    }
}
Nic.vector = 3; // distinct from kernel.bl0's Keyboard.vector=2 and Console.sysWriteVector=1
Nic.txQueue = [];
Nic.rxQueue = [];
Nic.rxHead = 0;
Nic.txCount = 0;
Nic.rxCount = 0;
Nic.hostBridgePresentPort = 3602; // set to 1 by the host when it attaches a bridge (NicFrame.HOST_BRIDGE_PRESENT_PORT)
Nic.hostSendVector = 5;           // distinct from 1 (console), 2 (keyboard), 3 (nic), 4 (host rx)
Nic.lock = newMutex();
Nic.rxEvent = newEvent();
Nic.dropNext = 0;

Nic.hostBridgeEnabled = false;
Nic.maxFrameBytes = 1500; // a conventional Ethernet-ish MTU - plenty for this toy stack's own headers+payload
Nic.hostRxVector = 4;     // distinct from Nic.vector=3 above
Nic.hostRxLenPort = 100;
Nic.hostRxDataBase = 102;      // 102 .. 102+maxFrameBytes-1
Nic.hostRxSeqPort = 1700;
Nic.hostRxAckPort = 1702;
Nic.hostTxSeqPort = 2000;
Nic.hostTxLenPort = 2002;
Nic.hostTxDataBase = 2004;     // 2004 .. 2004+maxFrameBytes-1
Nic.hostTxAckPort = 3600;
Nic.txSeq = 0;

// the "device" ISR: loops whatever was just staged back into the RX queue
def onNicIRQ(vector) {
    lock(Nic.lock);
    batch = Nic.txQueue;
    Nic.txQueue = [];
    i = 0;
    while (i < len(batch)) {
        push(Nic.rxQueue, batch[i]);
        Nic.rxCount = Nic.rxCount + 1;
        signalEvent(Nic.rxEvent);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    unlock(Nic.lock);
}

// ISR for a host-bridged frame: reads length + bytes off the RX ports (see
// this file's own header comment on the port layout) and pushes the
// reconstructed frame onto the SAME rxQueue Nic.recv() already drains -
// everything above this layer (ip.bl0/udp.bl0/tcp.bl0) can't tell a
// bridged frame from a looped-back one, they're both just "a frame that
// showed up in rxQueue". Acks hostRxSeqPort back on hostRxAckPort once
// the frame is fully read - the bridge (Java) blocks on that before
// injecting anything else, the RX-side counterpart to Nic.hostSend()'s own
// TX-side wait (see that method's own doc for why: with no queue on
// either side, two injections close together would otherwise race, the
// second overwriting the first before this ISR ever saw it).
def onHostNicIRQ(vector) {
    n = in16(Nic.hostRxLenPort);
    frame = [];
    i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        push(frame, in8(Nic.hostRxDataBase + i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    lock(Nic.lock);
    push(Nic.rxQueue, frame);
    Nic.rxCount = Nic.rxCount + 1;
    signalEvent(Nic.rxEvent);
    unlock(Nic.lock);
    out16(Nic.hostRxAckPort, in16(Nic.hostRxSeqPort));
}

// syscall handler (runs elevated, whatever ring the caller is in): relays one frame to the host bridge
def onHostSendSyscall(frame) {
    Nic.hostSend(frame);
}
